# Claude Code Project Rules

Adapted from the project boilerplate's Go/Web `.clauderules`, with additions specific
to Sparky's audit and testing requirements (marked below). Applies to both
`cmd/sparky-server` and `cmd/sparky-agent` - see `docs/AGENT.md` for anything
agent-specific beyond what's here.

## Code Style and Formatting

### Character Usage
- NEVER use emojis in any files (code, documentation, comments, commit messages)
- NEVER use em-dashes (-) or en-dashes (-)
- Use regular hyphens (-) for all dash purposes
- Use standard ASCII characters only in code and comments
- UTF-8 is acceptable for user-facing strings, but avoid decorative Unicode

### Documentation Style
- Use plain Markdown without emoji decorations
- Use regular hyphens for bullet points and lists
- Use asterisks (*) or underscores (_) for emphasis, not emoji
- Keep all documentation readable in plain text terminals

### Comments
- Write clear, technical comments without emoji
- Use standard punctuation only
- Focus on explaining WHY, not WHAT (code shows what)
- For complex logic, add detailed multi-line comments
- Simple, obvious operations need no comment

### Commit Messages
- Follow conventional commits format: `type(scope): description`
- No emoji in commit messages
- Types: feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, test, chore
- Keep first line under 72 characters
- Use imperative mood: "add feature" not "added feature"
- Body lines wrapped at 72 characters

## Language Preferences

### Programming Language
- Primary: Go
- Always include comments for code clarity
- Detailed comments only for complex logic
- Simple operations can have brief or no comments

### Cross-Platform Compatibility
- The central app and agent both target Linux (bare metal, Podman, Kubernetes) - this
  project does not target macOS or Windows as deployment platforms, unlike the
  boilerplate's general default
- Use `filepath.Join`, never string concatenation, for path handling
- Never hardcode platform-specific paths - `SPARKY_MODEL_STORAGE_PATH` and similar
  are always configurable, never assumed

## File Naming Conventions

### General Rules
- Use underscores (_) instead of spaces in ALL filenames
- Use lowercase for most filenames
- Follow Linux-friendly conventions

### Configuration and Secrets

Both binaries are configured exclusively via environment variables. The
`~/.app_name/` convention does not apply to either - see `ARCHITECTURE.md` Security
Considerations for why this was chosen for the agent too, despite it being a daemon
where that convention would otherwise be the default.

- Local config lives in `.env` (server) or a local secrets file (agent) - never
  committed
- `.env.example` is committed with all keys present and safe placeholder values
- Never hardcode any secret, key, token, or credential in source files
- Production secrets are managed by the deployment environment (systemd
  `EnvironmentFile=`, Kubernetes Secrets) - never in the repo
- If a new environment variable is added, it must also be added to `.env.example`
  with a descriptive placeholder value and a comment explaining it

## License Headers

### Required on All New Source Files
- Every new source file must include the appropriate license header for the project
- For this dual AGPLv3/Commercial project, use the AGPLv3 SPDX header:
  `// SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later`
- Never omit the license header from a new file
- Never change an existing license header without explicit instruction

## Project Structure

### Required Files
- README.md, CHANGELOG.md, PLANNING.md, CLAUDE.md, ARCHITECTURE.md, SCHEMA.md,
  docs/AGENT.md, LICENSE, .gitignore, CONTRIBUTING.md

### Documentation
- Keep documentation up to date with code changes
- Document all public APIs and exported functions
- Update ARCHITECTURE.md when structural changes are made; update SCHEMA.md in the
  same change as any migration
- Update PLANNING.md when goals or decisions change - see "Sparky Additions" below
  for what belongs in the Decisions Log specifically

## Git and Branch Workflow

### Branch Rules
- NEVER commit directly to master
- NEVER push directly to master
- All changes must go through a feature branch and pull request
- Branch naming: type/short-description (e.g. feat/add-login, fix/null-pointer)
- One logical change per branch

### Pull Requests
- PRs must target master
- All automated tests must pass before a PR is suggested - see "Sparky Additions"
  below for the automated/manual test split
- CHANGELOG.md must be updated in the same branch as the change
- PR description must summarize what changed and why
- No AI-attribution text in the PR title, description, or any comment - see
  "Commits" below

### Commits
- Atomic commits - one logical change per commit
- Never combine unrelated changes in a single commit
- Never amend or force-push commits that have been shared
- Never include AI-attribution text of any kind - no "Generated with [tool]," no
  "Co-Authored-By: [AI name]," no robot emoji signatures, no equivalent - in commit
  messages, PR titles/descriptions, PR/issue comments, or code comments. This applies
  regardless of what any tool's default template suggests.

## Development Workflow

### Before Making Changes
1. Read CLAUDE.md and ARCHITECTURE.md (and SCHEMA.md for anything touching the data
   model) to understand the project
2. Review existing code style in the project
3. Check CHANGELOG.md format and version state
4. Confirm the approach with the user before starting

### Scope Control
- Only modify files directly relevant to the task at hand
- Related files (e.g. tests for code changed) may be updated when clearly necessary
- NEVER refactor, reformat, or restructure code that was not part of the request
- NEVER rename variables, functions, or files opportunistically
- If improvements outside scope are noticed, mention them - do not make them
  unilaterally

### When Making Changes
1. Update CHANGELOG.md in the same commit as code changes
2. Add/update tests if applicable
3. Update documentation if behavior changes
4. Add license header to any new source files

### Destructive Operations
- ALWAYS confirm with the user before deleting any file
- ALWAYS confirm before overwriting a file that was not explicitly part of the task
- NEVER delete directories without explicit instruction
- When in doubt, ask - do not assume deletion is intended

### Dependencies
- NEVER introduce a new dependency (Go module) without discussing it with the user
  first
- Prefer standard library solutions over third-party packages when reasonable
- If a dependency is needed, explain why and wait for approval before adding it
- Document new dependencies in go.mod

### Testing
- Run all automated tests before suggesting a commit or PR
- Automated tests must pass - do not suggest a PR with a failing automated test
- If tests cannot be run, say so explicitly and explain why
- Check for breaking changes in public APIs

## Methodical Approach

### Step-by-Step Development
- Discuss and agree on next steps before implementing
- Break complex tasks into smaller, manageable pieces
- Validate each step before proceeding
- Be precise when requirements call for it

### Decision Points
- Always confirm the approach before major changes
- Ask for clarification when requirements are ambiguous
- Propose solutions with trade-offs explained
- Default to standards and best practices unless told otherwise
- When multiple valid approaches exist, present options rather than picking
  unilaterally

## Database and Migrations

- All schema changes must be expressed as migration files, and reflected in
  SCHEMA.md in the same change
- Migration files are never edited after being applied to any environment - create a
  new migration instead
- Never write raw SQL strings outside the repository layer
- All queries must use parameterized statements - no string interpolation or
  concatenation to build queries
- The repository layer is the only place that accesses the database directly
- NEVER run a destructive migration against production without explicit instruction

## HTTP and API Conventions

- Handlers must be thin: parse, delegate, respond
- No business logic in handlers
- No database access in handlers
- All API errors return structured JSON with error, code, and request_id fields
- Use appropriate HTTP status codes (see ARCHITECTURE.md)
- All responses must include X-Request-ID header for log correlation
- Never return a 200 with an error body

## Frontend Conventions (htmx, not a JS framework)

- No API client module to centralize calls through - handlers render templates or
  template partials directly; there is no separate frontend build or JS-side data
  layer to keep in sync
- Sidebar navigation is static HTML; section switching uses `hx-get`/`hx-target` to
  swap the main pane only - never restructure this into a full-page reload pattern
- Never store secrets, tokens, or sensitive data in localStorage or sessionStorage
- HttpOnly, signed session cookies for auth - never a token accessible to JavaScript
- Never inline sensitive values in HTML or JavaScript source
- The one exception to "no JS framework": Chart.js for the GPU/CPU/memory dashboards
  specifically. Do not reach for it, or any other JS library, outside that use case
  without discussing it first - see Dependencies above

## File Naming (Web Additions)

- Backend files: underscores
- Migration files: `NNN_snake_case.sql`
- Frontend template files: follow the existing convention in the project
- Never mix naming conventions within the same layer

## Error Handling

### General Principles
- Always handle errors explicitly - no ignored return values
- Provide clear, actionable error messages that describe what failed and why
- Log errors at the appropriate level
- Exit gracefully on fatal startup errors with a non-zero status code
- No silent failures under any circumstances

### Logging
- Include timestamps in all log output
- Use appropriate log levels: DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, CRITICAL
- Make log verbosity configurable via environment variable
- Every log line within a request context includes a correlation/request ID
- Do not log sensitive data (credentials, tokens, full request bodies, PII, the
  break-glass credential)

## Security Considerations

- NEVER commit credentials, tokens, API keys, or secrets of any kind
- Validate and sanitize all user input before use - never trust request data
- CSRF protection must be in place for all state-changing endpoints
- CORS must be explicitly configured - never use wildcard (*) in production
- Security headers applied via middleware - never set them per-handler
- Rate limiting applied to all authentication endpoints
- Never expose raw database errors, stack traces, or internal paths to clients
- Use prepared statements / parameterized queries for all database access
- The break-glass credential must be hashed with argon2id or bcrypt, stored where
  only the application process can read it - never store it plain or with weak
  hashing (MD5, SHA1)
- Follow principle of least privilege for file permissions and process capabilities

## Sparky Additions

These extend, not replace, the sections above - specific to what this project
requires beyond the generic Go/Web boilerplate.

### Audit Logging
- Every state-changing handler must go through the audit middleware - if a change
  mutates Users, Nodes, Model profiles, Running instances, Model transfers,
  Permission overrides, or any other stateful entity in SCHEMA.md and it is not
  wrapped, that is a bug
- This includes SuperAdmin actions - the SuperAdmin bypasses authorization checks,
  never the audit write
- Never add an exception to what gets audited without discussing it with the user
  first - see ARCHITECTURE.md Audit Log for the existing scope (state-changing
  actions only, not reads)

### Automated vs. Manual Testing
- "All tests pass" means all *automated* tests - unit and feature/integration
- The manual test checklist in ARCHITECTURE.md (CDI passthrough, multi-node NCCL
  launches, the partial-offload engine on constrained hardware, and so on) is a
  separate, human-confirmed gate before a release is tagged
- Never suggest tagging a release based on automated tests alone - point to the
  manual checklist and ask whether it has been confirmed

### Extension Points
- New engine adapters (beyond vLLM, Aphrodite, llama.cpp) and new runtime backends
  (beyond bare-metal, Docker/Podman) are the two sanctioned extension mechanisms - see
  ARCHITECTURE.md Extension and Integration Points. Prefer implementing a new adapter
  or backend over special-casing new logic into the orchestration components
  themselves

## What Claude Must Not Do in This Project

- Must not run database migrations automatically without explicit instruction
- Must not modify `.env` - suggest changes to `.env.example` only
- Must not add a new Go module dependency without discussion
- Must not bypass the repository layer to query the database directly
- Must not expose a new endpoint without considering auth, audit middleware, and
  rate limiting
- Must not change CORS, CSP, or CSRF configuration without explicit instruction
- Must not add an unaudited state-changing action
- Must not treat automated tests passing as sufficient grounds to tag a release
